      <h1>TPI vs PGA Golf Assessment: <em>Why You May Want Both</em></h1>
      <p>A PGA professional looks at your swing. A TPI trained practitioner looks at your body. Here is how they differ, and when combining both can make sense.</p>
      <p class="byline">By WellFinder · September 2026 · 5 min read</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-wrap">
        <div class="article-body">

<p>You booked a golf lesson.</p>
<p>Your PGA professional tells you to rotate more, stay connected, or change your backswing.</p>
<p>You understand the instruction.</p>
<p>But your body does not seem to cooperate.</p>
<p>This is where a TPI based assessment can add another layer.</p>
<p>A PGA golf professional primarily looks at the golf swing and helps you improve how you use the club.</p>
<p>A TPI trained practitioner can look at the relationship between your physical capabilities and your swing.</p>
<p>Neither approach has to replace the other.</p>
<p>In fact, the most useful approach for some golfers may be to understand both the swing problem and the physical limitation that may be contributing to it.</p>
<p>TPI itself describes a team approach involving golf, fitness, and medical professionals. (MyTPI)</p>

<figure class="article-figure"><img src="https://static1.squarespace.com/static/69af0664d044535c552e8a13/t/69c2bb2bd656800678cc8f13/1774369579353/Golf+Swing+Face+On.jpg?format=1500w" alt="A golfer mid swing indoors on pressure plates in a chiropractic golf clinic" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A golfer mid swing on pressure plates at Dr. Cole Baldigara, Victoria, where chiropractic rehab meets golf performance. Photo: <a href="https://drcolegolf.ca/golf-lesson-victoria" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dr. Cole Baldigara</a></figcaption></figure>

<h2>What does a PGA golf assessment focus on?</h2>
<p>A PGA professional is trained to help you play better golf.</p>
<p>Your assessment may focus on your:</p>
<ul>
<li>Grip</li>
<li>Setup</li>
<li>Alignment</li>
<li>Posture</li>
<li>Backswing</li>
<li>Downswing</li>
<li>Impact</li>
<li>Club path</li>
<li>Club face</li>
<li>Ball flight</li>
<li>Distance</li>
<li>Consistency</li>
</ul>
<p>Your instructor may use video, launch monitor data, club data, or other tools to understand what your club and ball are doing.</p>
<p>The outcome is generally a golf instruction plan.</p>
<p>You might leave with drills to practice at the range or at home.</p>
<p>This can be exactly what you need if your main problem is technical.</p>

<h2>What does a TPI assessment focus on?</h2>
<p>TPI approaches the golf swing through the relationship between the body and the swing.</p>
<p>A TPI physical screen looks at physical capabilities and considers how those findings may relate to swing characteristics. (MyTPI)</p>
<p>Your assessment may explore things such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Mobility</li>
<li>Stability</li>
<li>Balance</li>
<li>Strength</li>
<li>Movement control</li>
<li>Rotation</li>
<li>Physical limitations</li>
<li>Previous injuries</li>
</ul>
<p>The results can help determine whether a physical limitation may be relevant to your swing.</p>
<p>TPI offers separate certification pathways for golf, fitness, and medical professionals, so a TPI assessment can look different depending on who provides it. (MyTPI)</p>

<h2>TPI led vs PGA led</h2>
<p>It is more useful to think about these as different lenses rather than deciding that one is better.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th></th><th>TPI based assessment</th><th>PGA golf assessment</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th>Main focus</th><td>Body and swing connection</td><td>Golf swing and ball flight</td></tr>
<tr><th>Looks at</th><td>Physical capabilities and movement</td><td>Swing mechanics and club delivery</td></tr>
<tr><th>Typical provider</th><td>TPI Certified medical, fitness, or golf professional</td><td>PGA golf professional</td></tr>
<tr><th>May include</th><td>Movement screens and physical testing</td><td>Swing analysis and golf technology</td></tr>
<tr><th>Main outcome</th><td>Identify physical areas to work on</td><td>Identify swing changes and practice drills</td></tr>
<tr><th>Insurance</th><td>May be eligible when provided as an eligible health service, depending on provider and plan</td><td>Generally an out of pocket golf service</td></tr>
<tr><th>Best used for</th><td>Understanding physical limitations</td><td>Improving golf technique</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The important point is that TPI is not synonymous with physiotherapy.</p>
<p>A TPI Certified golf professional and a TPI Certified physiotherapist have different professional backgrounds.</p>
<p>Likewise, being a PGA professional does not prevent someone from having TPI training.</p>
<p>The useful question is not simply:</p>
<p>"Is this TPI or PGA?"</p>
<p>Ask:</p>
<p>"What does this professional specialize in, and what question am I trying to answer?"</p>

<h2>When should you start with TPI?</h2>
<p>A TPI based assessment may make sense if your golf problem feels physical.</p>
<p>For example, perhaps:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your backswing feels restricted.</li>
<li>You cannot comfortably rotate.</li>
<li>You lose balance during your swing.</li>
<li>One side feels significantly weaker.</li>
<li>You have returned to golf after an injury.</li>
<li>You have received swing instructions but physically struggle to make the change.</li>
</ul>
<p>In those situations, understanding your physical capabilities may give you useful information before you spend months trying to force a technical change.</p>
<p>TPI's approach specifically emphasizes assessing physical capabilities rather than assuming what a golfer can or cannot do. (MyTPI)</p>

<h2>When should you start with a PGA professional?</h2>
<p>If you are healthy and physically comfortable but your ball flight is inconsistent, a golf professional may be the natural starting point.</p>
<p>Maybe you are hitting a slice.</p>
<p>Maybe your driver launch is inconsistent.</p>
<p>Maybe your contact is poor.</p>
<p>Maybe you want more distance.</p>
<p>Maybe you are preparing for your first tournament.</p>
<p>These are fundamentally golf performance questions.</p>
<p>A PGA professional can observe your swing and help translate the problem into a practical golf adjustment.</p>

<h2>Why combining both can be useful</h2>
<p>This is where things get interesting.</p>
<p>Imagine your golf instructor tells you that you need more rotation in your backswing.</p>
<p>You try.</p>
<p>You cannot do it comfortably.</p>
<p>Instead of repeatedly forcing the movement, a TPI trained practitioner could assess whether your physical capabilities are limiting the movement.</p>
<p>If the assessment identifies a mobility or strength issue, you can work on that physical area.</p>
<p>Then you can return to your golf professional and work on the swing change again.</p>
<p>The two professionals are answering different questions.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>PGA:</strong> What is happening to my golf swing?</li>
<li><strong>TPI clinical assessment:</strong> What can my body currently do, and could that be influencing my swing?</li>
<li><strong>Training or rehabilitation:</strong> How can I improve the physical limitation?</li>
<li><strong>PGA coaching:</strong> How do I turn that improvement into a better golf swing?</li>
</ul>
<p>TPI explicitly promotes this type of collaboration between golf, fitness, and medical professionals. (MyTPI)</p>

<figure class="article-figure"><img src="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/69af0664d044535c552e8a13/7d6757ed-8788-4dbf-84cf-14dfc9eefbac/Med+Ball+Throw.webp?format=1000w" alt="A golfer performing a seated medicine ball power exercise in a golf clinic" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A medicine ball power exercise in a golf clinic setting at Dr. Cole Baldigara, Victoria. Photo: <a href="https://drcolegolf.ca/golf-lesson-victoria" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dr. Cole Baldigara</a></figcaption></figure>

<h2>A practical weekly golf plan</h2>
<p>You do not need to see every professional every week.</p>
<p>A simple routine could look like this:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monday:</strong> TPI based mobility or strength session</li>
<li><strong>Wednesday:</strong> Golf practice with specific drills</li>
<li><strong>Saturday:</strong> PGA golf lesson or playing session</li>
<li><strong>Sunday:</strong> Light mobility and recovery</li>
</ul>
<p>You could also use a clinical assessment periodically rather than continuously.</p>
<p>For example, you might start the season with a TPI assessment, work on your physical limitations for several weeks, and then have a PGA lesson to apply the changes to your swing.</p>
<p>If pain appears, stop treating it as simply a swing problem and consider getting appropriate clinical advice.</p>

<h2>And what about your benefits?</h2>
<p>This is another reason to understand the difference.</p>
<p>A PGA golf lesson is generally a golf instruction expense.</p>
<p>A TPI assessment provided by a physiotherapist or chiropractor may potentially fit within your extended health coverage if the service is eligible under that practitioner's benefit category and your plan.</p>
<p>For chiropractic care, the Ontario Chiropractic Association notes that many extended health plans cover chiropractic services, but the insurer determines eligibility under the specific plan. (OCA)</p>
<p>For physiotherapy, the same principle applies: the fact that an assessment uses a TPI framework does not automatically make it a covered physiotherapy service.</p>
<p>Your professional credentials and the way the service is billed matter.</p>
<p>Ask before booking.</p>

<h2>The simple way to think about it</h2>
<p>You do not have to choose between TPI and PGA.</p>
<p>Think of them as two different pieces of your golf development.</p>
<p>TPI can help you understand the body behind the swing.</p>
<p>PGA instruction can help you understand and change the swing itself.</p>
<p>And when the right professionals work together, you can connect the two.</p>
<p>For many golfers, that means fewer guesses about why a swing change feels impossible and a clearer plan for what to work on next.</p>

<figure class="article-figure"><img src="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/69af0664d044535c552e8a13/a77085d0-acef-40f2-bd4e-bb99f13b340d/Shoulder+Ext+Rot.webp?format=1000w" alt="A practitioner measuring a clients shoulder rotation with a goniometer" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A practitioner measuring shoulder rotation at Dr. Cole Baldigara, Victoria. Assessing physical capabilities first can make later swing work more productive. Photo: <a href="https://drcolegolf.ca/golf-lesson-victoria" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dr. Cole Baldigara</a></figcaption></figure>

